  "text": "Allergies:\nNo Known Allergies / Adverse Drug Reactions\n\nChief Complaint:\nTachycardia\n\nHistory of Present Illness:\n___ year old male with a history of DVT/PE in ___ in the setting \nof surgery for a left ankle fracture, atrial fibrillation, \npossible embolic TIA, hypertension, and asymmetric septal \nhypertrophy presenting to ED from outpatient pulmonary stress \ntest with tachycardia. Patient presents with tachycarida \n160s-170s.  \nAs per medical records and patient description, the patients \nhistory for atrial fibrillation and treatment has briefly been \noutlined below:  \nPatient has a history of having a PE after ankle surgery and \ndeveloping A.fib. There was concern he may have had a embolic \nTIA in ___ however work up with carotid ultrasound was \nnegative. Patient was started on metoprolol and lisinopril for \nHTN as he was in sinus rhythm after that event. In ___ he was \nnoted to have persistent a.fib In that setting he had an \nechocardiogram done which showed an EF of 35%. He underwent \nsuccessful DCCV and was started on flecainde. This was \ncomplicated post DCCV pulmonary edema which resolved with lasix. \nThe patient had recurrent paroxysmal atrial fibrillation in \nspite of increased flecainide dose.  \n \nPatient then discontinued metoprolol as he thought it may be \ncontributing to his fatigue. In ___ he was in sinus rhythm \nin spite of being off flecainide. The decision was made to \ncontinue him on the lower dose of metoprolol and to remain off \nantiarrhthmics. He was also to remain on coumadin for \nthromboembolic prophylaxis.  \n \nIn ___ of this year he was seen at a hospital in ___ where \nhe was found to have a supra therapeutic INR. He was also noted \nto be in atrial fibrillation with ventricular rates in the 190s. \nHe underwent DCCV and was started on Multaq 400mg BID. He states \nthat immediately following restoration of sinus rhythm and the \nfew days following discharge he overall felt very well. Since \nthen however, he continues to have periods of significant \nfatigue and dyspnea on exertion.  \nToday, patient presented to outpatient pulmonary stress test \nwhere he was noted to have HR in the 160-170 range without \nsymptoms prompting referal to the ED. Of note, he reports \nmedical non-compliance for the past week as he states he feels \nunwell on his home antiarrhythmics. Patient remains in atrial \nfibrillation in spite of the Multaq. He has not tolerated being \non the digoxin, diltiazem, and Multaq combination ___ side \neffects. Plan from outpatient was to attempt atrial fibrillation \nablation. Patient notes that he has had ongoing dyspnea on \nexertion for the last several months but denies orthopnea, PND. \nHe denies CP, cough, abdominal pain, peripheral edema.  \n \nIn the ED intial vitals were: 98.2 102 145/89 16 95%  \nLabs were notable for normal CBC, ___ elevated at 24.9, PTT \nelevated at 43.9, and INR of 2.3. Chem 7 was normal.  \nDuring ED course, aptient with persistent afib with rates in the \n130s-160s.  \n \nPatient was given: Dilt 20 with no effect Dilt 25 with minimal \neffect, and they started start dilt gtt at rate of 15.  \n \nOn the floor, patient remains in afib with rates ranging \n130-150. Vitals 97.9 129/83 149 16 94%RA.  \nROS: On review of systems, s/he denies any prior history of \nstroke, TIA, deep venous thrombosis, pulmonary embolism, \nbleeding at the time of surgery, myalgias, joint pains, cough, \nhemoptysis, black stools or red stools. S/he denies recent \nfevers, chills or rigors. S/he denies exertional buttock or calf \npain. All of the other review of systems were negative.  \nCardiac review of systems is notable for absence of chest pain, \ndyspnea on exertion, paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnea, orthopnea, \nankle edema, palpitations, syncope or presyncope.\n\nBrief Hospital Course:\n___ y M with PMH sig for refractory Afib, HTN, and asymmetric \nseptal hypertrophy presenting to ED from outpatient pulmonary \nstress test with tachycardia 160s-170s in the setting of not \ntaking his afib medications (has been taking coumadin and \nlisinopril) for 2 weeks.\n \nACTIVE ISSUES\n===============\n#Afib with RVR\nThe patient has a long hx of Afib with RVR. Has failed treatment \nin past with multiple meds and prior cardioversions, hasn't \ntaken meds for 2 weeks. He was sent to the ED with asymptomatic \nwith HRs 140's-160's.  In the ED, he was treated with a \ndiltiazem gtt; however, his rate did not come down with dilt \ndrip.  Upon admission the floor, he was transitioned to low dose \nPO dilt.  He was taken for cardioversion on ___ with \nsuccessful cardioversion into sinus rhythm.  He was discharged \non Quinadine 324mg PO TID as well as Verapamil SR 120 mg PO \nQ24H.  He will plan to return for MRI and PVI and was schedule \nfor followup with Dr. ___.  Coumadin was continued based \non home doses.\n \nCHRONIC ISSUES\n================\n# HTN: \nHome dose lisinopril continued. \n\nTRANSITIONAL ISSUES\n=====================\n- Successful electrical cardioversion of atrial fibrillation to\nsinus rhythm on ___\n- will need to return for MRI and PVI\n- new medications include: Quinadine 324mg PO TID as well as \nVerapamil SR 120 mg PO Q24H \n- patient will continue his home dose coumadin, to be followed \nper patient, by Dr. ___\n\n \n___ on Admission:\nThe Preadmission Medication list is accurate and complete.\n1. Lisinopril 5 mg PO DAILY \n2. Warfarin 5 mg PO 5X/WEEK (___) \n3. Warfarin 7.5 mg PO 2X/WEEK (TH)\n\nDischarge Diagnosis:\nPRIMARY\nAtrial Fibrillation\n\nSECONDARY\nHypertension",
